; ── Restless spirit (raised if someone tries to force the chest) ─────────────
; Non-hostile (defends itself only), cannot be harmed by ordinary weapons, and
; vanishes when the chest opens (ZAPMON in MACRO 9301). GMs can @zap them too.
MNUMBER 9300
NAME spirit
ADJECTIVE 9300
HUMAN
*DESCRIPTION_START
The pale, sorrowful shade of one of Old Fayd's long dead. Its features are worn away like the runes on the gravestones, but its hollow eyes are fixed on the iron-bound chest -- and on you.
*DESCRIPTION_END
BODY 500
ATTACK1 20
DEFENSE 200
DISCORPORATE
ETERNAL
MAGICWEAPON 3
IMMUNITY 0 0
IMMUNITY 5 0
IMMUNITY 6 0
IMMUNITY 7 0
IMMUNITY 8 1
IMMUNITY 9 0
RACE 22
speed 3
STRATEGY 150
TREASURE 0
WEAPON 544 -1 10
TEXG A restless spirit rises silently from a nearby grave.
TEXE A restless spirit drifts in.
TEXM A restless spirit drifts
TEXI Your weapon passes through the restless spirit without resistance.
TEXD sighs and fades away.
TEX1 A restless spirit whispers, "Return what was taken..."
TEX2 A restless spirit turns its hollow eyes toward the graves.
TEX3 A restless spirit drifts slowly around the chest.

; ── Rooms ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

; 287 — the festival lot (copied from FALLFEST.SCR — keep the two in sync) plus
; the manifestation trigger and the rekindling of the fire.
NUMBER 287
NAME Poor Quarter, festival lot
OUTDOOR_OTHER
FIXED_LIGHT
CALL 33
*DESCRIPTION_START
Rubble has been cleared from the west end of this alley to make room for the Fall Festival. Strings of paper lanterns sway between the leaning tenements, and logs have been dragged into a ring around the campfire to serve as benches. A trestle table sags beneath pumpkin bread and a steaming kettle of hot cider, free to all, and a gaudy striped balloon cart is parked against one wall beside a velvet-draped stool where a crystal ball glimmers. Beyond the merriment, an imposing iron gate still seals off the Old Fayd Cemetery, its chains rattling softly whenever the wind finds them.
*DESCRIPTION_END
ITEM 0 219 VAL2=299
ITEM 1 692 val2=644 VAL5=1
ITEM 2 9360
ITEM 3 9361
ITEM 4 9362
ITEM 5 9363
ITEM 6 9364
ITEM 7 9365
ITEM 9 693 ADJ1=10
EXIT E 286
EXIT ABOVE 2152
*DESCRIPTION_START ITEM EXAM 3
A long trestle table covered in an orange cloth. A hand-lettered card propped against the kettle reads, "Help yourself! -- the Festival Committee." (GET BREAD or GET CIDER)
*DESCRIPTION_END
*DESCRIPTION_START ITEM EXAM 6
A wooden cart painted in loud stripes of orange and black, with dozens of balloons bobbing on strings tied to its rail. A price board is nailed to the side.
*DESCRIPTION_END
*DESCRIPTION_START ITEM READ 6
         ~ BALLOONS ~
  Orange, black, purple or white ..... 1 shilling
  TREASURE balloon ................... 1 crown
     (POP it and see what's inside!)
*DESCRIPTION_END
*DESCRIPTION_START ITEM EXAM 7
A crystal ball the size of a melon rests on a velvet-draped stool. Faint mist stirs inside it. A small sign reads, "Touch, and know thy fortune."
*DESCRIPTION_END
*DESCRIPTION_START ITEM EXAM 0
The iron gate is old and heavy, its bars wrapped in rusted chains. Beyond it, rows of broken gravestones fade into the dark of the Old Fayd Cemetery. The gate can still be climbed, if you dare.
*DESCRIPTION_END
STOREITEM 739 225 10
STOREITEM 739 25 10
STOREITEM 739 248 10
STOREITEM 739 347 10
STOREITEM 739 9350 100
; The campfire's look follows HWFIRE (its archetype changes with the story, so
; it has no static ITEM EXAM text).
IFPREVERB LOOK 2
  CLEARVERB
  IFVAR HWFIRE = 1
    ECHO PLAYER The campfire burns with a cold, pale blue flame that gives off no warmth at all. Every shadow around it leans toward the cemetery gate.
  ENDIF
  IFVAR HWFIRE = 2
    ECHO PLAYER Nothing is left of the campfire but cold grey ash inside the ring of stones. It is as cold as if it had been out for a hundred years.
  ENDIF
  IFVAR HWFIRE ! 1
    IFVAR HWFIRE ! 2
      ECHO PLAYER A cheerful fire of split logs crackles inside a ring of old grave-curbing stones. Sparks drift up into the dark and wink out among the paper lanterns.
    ENDIF
  ENDIF
ENDIF
; The manifestation. A GM arms it with @set HWLANTERN 1; then whoever says
; "...your vigil is done" here (Soulcatcher: "Marren Vey, your vigil is done.")
; sets it off. The speaker must not be @invis/@hide (that silences the echoes).
IFSAY YOUR_VIGIL_IS_DONE
  IFVAR HWLANTERN = 1
    EQUAL HWLANTERN 2
    EQUAL HWFIRE 1
    NEWITEM 2 9300
    ECHO ALL The campfire abruptly changes from a warm orange to a burning pale blue.
    PLREVENT 4
    CONTPLREVENT
    ECHO ALL Every shadow in the lot -- yours included -- slides across the ground toward the fire and turns to face the cemetery, no matter where the fire stands.
    PLREVENT 5
    CONTPLREVENT
    ECHO ALL Beyond the chained gate, a translucent man appears among the graves, holding a dark, hooded lantern.
    PLREVENT 5
    CONTPLREVENT
    ECHO ALL The ghost raises one withered hand and points into the cemetery.
    PLREVENT 5
    CONTPLREVENT
    ECHO ALL The ghost moans, "They raided the graves. They took my life. They did not take what I guarded. Follow the light -- and remember my name."
    PLREVENT 6
    CONTPLREVENT
    EQUAL HWFIRE 2
    NEWITEM 2 9301
    NEWITEM 8 9302 ADJ1=7
    ECHO ALL The apparition vanishes. With a long sigh, the pale flames gutter and die, leaving the campfire cold and dark. Something glints among the ashes: an ancient grave token.
    GMMSG [Last Lantern] Manifestation complete. Stage 2: the obelisk token is in the ashes; clue 1 (SEARCH SKULL at the obelisk, room 300) is live.
  ENDIF
ENDIF
; Closing: the fire rekindles by itself when someone returns after the chest
; has been opened.
IFENTRY
  IFVAR HWLANTERN = 8
    IFVAR HWFIRE = 2
      EQUAL HWFIRE 3
      NEWITEM 2 9360
      ECHO ALL With a soft whoomp, the dead campfire rekindles by itself. Its flames are warm and orange and ordinary once more.
    ENDIF
  ENDIF
ENDIF
;
; 299 — Cemetery, gate (FAYDFALL.SCR; MONSTER_GROUP removed).
NUMBER 299
NAME Cemetery, gate
DAY_LIGHT
FOREST
CALL 33
*DESCRIPTION_START
To the east a gate leads back to the safety of the city proper. A rough trail weaves southwest among rows of grave markers worn and broken by the passage of years. The runes engraved upon the stones are unreadable: their language lost to time. Brambles and weeds have overgrown the spaces between the gravestones and thick creepers coil wilting and drab tendrils tightly about the nearby gate as if it might shelter them from winter. Through the bars you can see the glow of the festival lanterns.
*DESCRIPTION_END
ITEM 0 219 VAL2=287
ITEM 1 337
ITEM 9 693 ADJ1=10

EXIT SW 300
;
; 300 — Cemetery, trail: the broken obelisk and the vine-choked skull (clue 1).
NUMBER 300
NAME Cemetery, trail
DAY_LIGHT
FOREST
CALL 33
*DESCRIPTION_START
The trail bends around a broken obelisk, and leads northeast towards to the gate, and westward into the deepening gloom of the cemetery. Withered vines grow through the eye sockets of a dirty skull laying beside the trail.
*DESCRIPTION_END
EXIT NE 299
EXIT W 301
EXIT S 3186
ITEM 0 352
ITEM 1 228 ADJ1=374
ITEM 9 693 ADJ1=10

*DESCRIPTION_START ITEM EXAM 0
The skull doesn't seem to be particularly old. Its presence here seems odd considering this cemetery has been deserted for over a thousand years. Dead vines have grown thickly through its eye sockets.
*DESCRIPTION_END
*DESCRIPTION_START ITEM EXAM 1
The obelisk has snapped off halfway up, and the jagged stump is furred with lichen. Whatever was carved on it has long since weathered away.
*DESCRIPTION_END
IFPREVERB SEARCH 1
  CLEARVERB
  IFVAR HWLANTERN < 2
    ECHO PLAYER You search around the base of the broken obelisk but find nothing but weeds and lichen.
  ELSE
    ECHO PLAYER Circling the base of the broken obelisk, you notice the dirty skull lying beside it -- and that the dead vines growing through its eye sockets have been disturbed, as though something was pushed inside.
    ECHO OTHERS %N searches around the base of the broken obelisk and peers at the skull beside it.
  ENDIF
ENDIF
IFPREVERB SEARCH 0
  CLEARVERB
  IFVAR HWLANTERN < 2
    ECHO PLAYER You poke at the dead vines around the skull but find nothing of interest.
  ELSE
    EQUAL DUMMY1 0
    IFCARRY 9303
      EQUAL DUMMY1 1
    ENDIF
    IFVAR DUMMY1 = 1
      ECHO PLAYER You have already taken the copper strip from inside the skull.
    ELSE
      ECHO PLAYER Working your fingers between the dead vines, you find something wedged inside the skull: a thin strip of copper, green with age, bearing the image of a leaf.
      ECHO OTHERS %N reaches into the skull's vine-choked eye socket and draws out a thin strip of copper.
      NEWITEM -1 9303
      EQUAL ROUNDTIME 5
      IFVAR HWLANTERN = 2
        EQUAL HWLANTERN 3
        GMMSG [Last Lantern] %N found the copper strip FIRST (leg 1).
      ENDIF
    ENDIF
  ENDIF
ENDIF
;
; 301 — Cemetery, trail: the hanging willow (FAYDFALL.SCR; MONSTER_GROUP removed).
NUMBER 301
NAME Cemetery, trail
FOREST
DAY_LIGHT
CALL 33
*DESCRIPTION_START
The stench of decay is undeniable. A number of decaying bodies swing lazily in the breeze from the branches of a gnarled willow tree. Dozens of crows roost in the higher branches. Occasionally one flits down to peck on a piece of the rotting flesh. They pay your passing little heed.
*DESCRIPTION_END
ITEM 0 229 ADJ1=146 ADJ2=385
ITEM 1 230 ADJ1=88
*DESCRIPTION_START ITEM EXAM 1
You don't see anyone you know, however it is clear that these corpses are rather fresh. One has the name Tayshan embroidered on what is left of a shirt pocket.
*DESCRIPTION_END
ITEM 9 693
EXIT E 300
EXIT N 309
EXIT SW 302
;
; 302 — Cemetery, trail: the empty grave (FAYDFALL.SCR; MONSTER_GROUP removed).
NUMBER 302
NAME Cemetery, trail
FOREST
DAY_LIGHT
CALL 33
*DESCRIPTION_START
Beside the trail is an empty grave. Who it was dug for is a mystery. Leaves cover a pile dry dirt next to the hole. Thorny undergrowth nearly prevents continued travel, but you can force your way through.
*DESCRIPTION_END
ITEM 0 231 VAL2=303
ITEM 9 693
EXIT NE 301
EXIT W 307
;
; 307 — outside the caretaker's house: the shovel and prybar.
NUMBER 307
NAME Cemetery, trail
DAY_LIGHT
FOREST
CALL 33
*DESCRIPTION_START
The trail ends before a crumbling granite structure. One wall has partially collapsed, allowing easy entry into its weed and bramble infested interior. A few dried leaves, scattered in the cold wind, cling to the stone. A narrow path climbs away from the trail, winding higher into the gravestones.
*DESCRIPTION_END
EXIT E 302
ITEM 0 83 VAL2=308 ADJ1=386
ITEM 1 492 ADJ1=533 ADJ2=148
ITEM 9 693
ITEM 2 8300 VAL2=5300
*DESCRIPTION_START ITEM EXAM 1
This crumbling granite structure looks as though it was once a small house, perhaps the cemetery caretaker's. Weeds grow thick against its walls.
*DESCRIPTION_END
; Any search here (bare SEARCH, SEARCH STRUCTURE, SEARCH WALL...) turns up the
; tools leaning against the house, once the story is under way. If they've been
; carried off, another search finds more junk of the same sort.
IFPREVERB SEARCH -1
  IFVAR HWLANTERN >= 1
    EQUAL DUMMY1 0
    IFNOITEM 3
      EQUAL DUMMY1 1
      NEWITEM 3 484 ADJ1=222
    ENDIF
    IFNOITEM 4
      EQUAL DUMMY1 1
      NEWITEM 4 9306
    ENDIF
    IFVAR DUMMY1 = 1
      CLEARVERB
      ECHO PLAYER Pushing aside the weeds along the outside of the house, you find an old shovel and a rusted prybar leaning against the wall, half hidden by brambles.
      ECHO OTHERS %N pushes aside the weeds along the wall of the ruined house and uncovers some old tools.
    ENDIF
  ENDIF
ENDIF
